  "final": "The CSV and JSON export now share one code path in the new `src/exporter.py`. `python3 checks/public.py` passes, including the checks I added.\n\n- **`src/exporter.py`**: `export_rows(rows, format)` does the row normalization once. It keeps only `id` and `name`, uses `''` for missing or `None` values, stringifies everything else and keeps row order. It builds a new list, so one-shot iterables work and the inputs aren't changed. It then serializes as `csv` or `json`, and any other format raises `ValueError(\"unsupported format\")`.\n  - **CSV**: LF line endings, standard quoting, and an `id,name` header even with no rows.\n  - **JSON**: `ensure_ascii=False` and `separators=(',', ':')`, with no trailing newline, as before.\n- **`src/csv_exporter.py` and `src/json_exporter.py`**: `export_csv` and `export_json` are now one-line wrappers around `export_rows`. Their own loops are gone.\n- **`src/dispatcher.py`**: `export(rows, format)` delegates to `export_rows`, so the `ValueError` message is unchanged.\n- **`checks/public.py`**: I added checks for:\n  - one-shot iterables;\n  - missing and `None` values;\n  - CSV quoting;\n  - non-ASCII JSON output;\n  - empty input for both formats;\n  - the unsupported-format error.\n\n`python` isn't installed in the workspace, only `python3`, so I ran everything with `python3`.",
